{"reference":"1 Chronicles 4","book":"1 Chronicles","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Genealogies","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The genealogical material in Chronicles can be read as a selective map of kinship, land, office, and restored identity, rather than as an exhaustive modern-style biological pedigree. That perspective may help explain why these lists include family lines, settlements, occupations, and occasional gaps or parallel names; individual relationships still require case-by-case reading.","verse_refs":["1 Chronicles 1:1-54","1 Chronicles 2:1-55","1 Chronicles 3:1-24","1 Chronicles 5:1-26","1 Chronicles 6:1-81","1 Chronicles 7:1-40","1 Chronicles 8:1-40","1 Chronicles 9:1-44"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_chronicles_004_edafcd369e4e738502d272c9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}